seed:
  rowsPerEntity:
    plan: 5
    customer: 70
    payment_method: 110
    subscription: 90
    invoice: 300
    payment: 380
    billing_event: 520
  mix: "All history is before 2026-10-06T09:00:00Z and all scheduled times are after it. Plans: Starter (900/month), Pro (4900/month), Business (19900/month), Pro Annual (49000/year) and one inactive legacy plan. Customers: 70, each with 1 to 2 cards. About 25% of cards use a failing token (a mix of tok_insufficient_funds, tok_expired_card and tok_chargeDeclined). Of the cards that are default for a customer with an active subscription, all use tok_visa. Subscriptions: 90, with roughly 55% active, 31% past_due (28 rows, so lists page past 25) and 14% canceled (a mix of requested and dunning_exhausted). The 28 past_due subscriptions sit at dunning stages attempt_count 1 (10), 2 (9) and 3 (9). Each has one open invoice with a consistent dunning_started_at, a future next_retry_at (1d, 3d or 7d after dunning_started_at) and a failure code. Four of the attempt_count 3 rows are on the Business plan, spread across list pages, and the others are on Starter or Pro. Active subscriptions have periods ending 1 to 29 days after the clock start. Invoices: paid history for each active subscription (1 to 6 invoices), one open invoice per past_due subscription, void or uncollectible invoices for canceled subscriptions. Payments: one succeeded payment per paid invoice and failed payments matching each past_due invoice's attempt_count. Named anchors: customers 'Harbor Dental' (active Pro subscription, cancel target) and 'Harbor Dentistry' (similar name, active subscription). 'Bluefin Labs' (past_due at attempt_count 1, failing default card, plus a second tok_visa card ending 4444 on file but not default). Events are derived from the invoices and payments in time order."
tasks:
  - id: cancel_harbor_dental_subscription
    difficulty: easy
    intent: The customer Harbor Dental asks to cancel their subscription. Find the customer by name and cancel its one active subscription with the cancel action. The grader checks the subscription is canceled with reason requested and that nothing else changed apart from its events and invoices. Harbor Dentistry, with a near-identical name, must stay untouched.
    decoyIdea: Cancel the subscription of the similarly named customer Harbor Dentistry, or cancel the first search hit without checking the exact name.
  - id: recover_bluefin_labs_with_backup_card
    difficulty: medium
    intent: Bluefin Labs' subscription is past due because its default card keeps failing. They have a second card ending in 4444 already on file. Make that card the customer's default, then collect the open invoice now. The grader checks the default is the 4444 card, the invoice is paid by a succeeded payment on that card, the subscription is active again, and no other customer's rows changed. A manual payment must not create extra retry attempts.
    decoyIdea: Pay the invoice before switching the default card, so the manual charge fails on the old card. Or switch the default card and wait for the retry job, which agents cannot trigger. Or pay with a card of a different customer, or the wrong card ending.
  - id: cancel_final_retry_business_subscriptions
    difficulty: hard
    intent: Finance wants to stop dunning for the biggest accounts about to take their last retry. Cancel every subscription on the Business plan that is past_due and whose open invoice is on its third attempt (attempt_count 3, so only the 7-day retry is left). The past_due list spans more than one page. Other past_due subscriptions, such as Business ones at earlier stages and non-Business ones at attempt 3, must stay untouched. The grader checks each target is canceled with reason requested and its invoice is void, and that every other row is unchanged.
    decoyIdea: Read only the first page of past_due subscriptions. Cancel every past_due Business subscription regardless of attempt_count. Cancel all attempt_count 3 invoices regardless of plan. Or pay the invoices instead of cancelling.
open_questions:
  - question: Are the retry days 1, 3 and 7 measured from the first failure, or from the previous retry?
    default_answer: From the first failure. Retries happen on day 1, day 3 and day 7.
  - question: What happens to the subscription when the last retry fails?
    default_answer: Cancelled outright, with cancel_reason dunning_exhausted. The invoice becomes uncollectible.
  - question: Should the subscription stay active while dunning runs, or become past_due?
    default_answer: It becomes past_due on the first failed charge and returns to active on any successful payment.
  - question: Does a manual payment attempt count as a dunning attempt?
    default_answer: No. Manual attempts are recorded but do not change attempt_count or the retry schedule.
  - question: Should the default_card workflow's derived states be a stored state field on customer?
    default_answer: No. Declare a descriptive lifecycle on the workflow and change nothing in the world.
assumptions:
  - decision: clock.start is 2026-10-06T09:00:00.000Z with tick 0s. All seeded history precedes it. All next_retry_at and current_period_end values in the seed are after it.
    why: Time moves only on explicit advance, so dunning tests can assert exact timestamps.
  - decision: 'Retry days (1, 3, 7) are counted from the first failed charge (dunning_started_at). That gives four attempts: day 0, 1, 3 and 7.'
    why: Stripe-style smart retries offset from the original failure.
  - decision: If the day-7 retry fails, the invoice becomes uncollectible and the subscription is canceled with cancel_reason dunning_exhausted.
    why: A distinct reason lets agents tell it from a customer-requested cancel.
  - decision: Retries and renewals run in hourly jobs.
    why: Hourly granularity is realistic and deterministic.
  - decision: Card outcomes are deterministic through the test token on each payment_method.
    why: The world must be reproducible.
  - decision: The retry job charges whichever card is the customer's default at retry time.
    why: Mirrors real dunning and gives agents a fix action.
  - decision: Manual pay_invoice failures are recorded as kind manual and do not change attempt_count or next_retry_at.
    why: Manual collection must not accelerate cancellation.
  - decision: A failed first charge at subscribe time still creates the subscription (past_due, invoice open) and answers 201.
    why: A single past_due state keeps the lifecycle small.
  - decision: A month is 30 days and a year is 365 days. Renewal periods are measured from the previous period end.
    why: Time helpers support only d/h/m/s offsets.
  - decision: USD only, integer cents, no tax, proration, coupons, trials or line items.
    why: Not needed for dunning behaviour.
  - decision: Plan changes take effect on the next invoice with no proration.
    why: Simplest consistent behaviour.
  - decision: Cancellation is always immediate, voids an open invoice, and paid invoices stay paid.
    why: Dunning needs a clear terminal state.
  - decision: A customer cannot hold two non-canceled subscriptions to the same plan (409 duplicate_subscription).
    why: Avoids duplicate billing.
  - decision: The default card is changed only through set_default_payment_method.
    why: Forces the explicit step dunning recovery depends on.
  - decision: Failed pay_invoice charges answer 200 with the failed payment.
    why: A declined card is business data that must persist.
  - decision: 'List endpoints use the default envelope: data and next_cursor. Errors use {error:{code,message}}.'
    why: Follows engine defaults.
  - decision: The default_card workflow gets lifecycle representation descriptive, because its states are derived from whether customer.default_payment_method_id is set and are not stored in a state field. Nothing else in the world changes and all seed counts stay as they are.
    why: The change request asks for this plan-only revision.
outOfScope:
  - what: Real card networks, 3-D Secure, card tokenisation and PCI concerns
    why: Outcomes are simulated by a test token so the world stays deterministic.
  - what: Taxes, discounts, coupons, credits, proration and multi-line invoices
    why: Not part of the requested dunning flow.
  - what: Free trials, paused subscriptions, cancel_at_period_end and subscription reactivation after cancellation
    why: They would add states beyond the three-state lifecycle.
  - what: Refunds, disputes and chargebacks
    why: No money moves back in this flow.
  - what: Customer-facing emails, webhooks and payment-failed notifications
    why: Agents act through the API. billing_event stands in for notifications.
  - what: Multi-currency, bank debits and wallets
    why: Cards in USD are enough.
  - what: Delete routes for any entity
    why: Billing records are kept for audit.
  - what: Configurable dunning schedule
    why: The 1, 3 and 7 day schedule is fixed.
changes:
  - workflows.default_card
  - tests.dunning_retries_at_1_3_7_days_then_cancels
  - tests.renewal_creates_next_invoice
